00:06:15.050 --> 00:06:20.050
return a typed answer plus a probability instead of generating free text and

00:06:20.050 --> 00:06:24.270
that is where I want to pop the hood for bounded tasks. Classification and

00:06:24.270 --> 00:06:28.270
judging no longer need a parser glued on the end because the label arrives

00:06:28.270 --> 00:06:33.050
already typed. In contact center workloads those models match frontier large

00:06:33.050 --> 00:06:39.010
language models, or LLMs, on six classification problems, and they do it at the lowest

00:06:39.010 --> 00:06:42.010
per-request cost among the API options tested.

00:06:42.610 --> 00:06:47.470
Abstention is the whole, most of them almost never picked the explicit no-answer option,

00:06:47.830 --> 00:06:51.110
and that is the widest gap against the strongest generative baselines.

00:06:51.690 --> 00:06:57.470
On two judging tasks, accuracy falls sharply once parameter count drops, and they show higher

00:06:57.470 --> 00:06:58.310
position bias.

00:06:58.890 --> 00:07:01.350
They lean on answer order than generative judges.

00:07:01.350 --> 00:07:08.090
8 real-world use cases point to a split, automatically accept high-confidence decision model predictions

00:07:08.530 --> 00:07:12.490
and send the uncertain remainder to generative LLMs or to a human.

00:07:13.110 --> 00:07:16.510
That routing produced the best accuracy cost trade-off in the study.

00:07:17.190 --> 00:07:21.750
High-volume intent classification and compliance checking are the workloads where a decision

00:07:21.750 --> 00:07:24.810
